==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*skulaną||to owe}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*skel-||shall, must, owe}}. Cognate with Old High German {{m|goh|scolan}} (German {{m|de|sollen}}), Old Saxon {{m|osx|skulan}}, Dutch {{m|nl|zullen}}, Old Norse {{m|non|skulu}} (Swedish {{m|sv|skola}}, Norwegian {{m|no|skulle}}), Gothic {{m|got|𐍃𐌺𐌿𐌻𐌰𐌽}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈʃulɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|head=sċulan}}

# {{lb|ang|transitive}} to [[owe]]
# {{lb|ang|auxiliary}} to be obliged, [[must]], [[should]]
#: ''Him æghwylc þara ymbsittendra ofer hronrade hyran scolde.'' All of the neighbouring tribes over the ocean had to listen to him. (Beowulf ll.9-10)
# {{lb|ang|auxiliary}} [[shall]], to be going or about (to do something due to some sort of obligation not intention)

